---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SlocumDecoder from '../../components/SlocumDecoder.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Slocum glider decoder"
  description="Decode Slocum glider binary data — sbd, tbd, mbd, nbd, dbd, ebd and their compressed forms — to CSV or netCDF, entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."
>
  <div class="container">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/data/">Data &amp; Tools</a></p>
    <h1>Slocum glider decoder</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      Teledyne Webb's Slocum gliders log to a binary format with no published
      specification. This reads it — flight and science files, compressed or
      not — and writes CSV or netCDF. It runs entirely in your browser: the
      files are never uploaded, which matters while a deployment is still under
      embargo.
    </p>

    <SlocumDecoder />

    <section class="about">
      <h2>What you need</h2>
      <p>
        Two things: the data files, and the <strong>sensor-list cache</strong> they refer to.
      </p>
      <p>
        A file off a glider is <em>factored</em> — it names an eight-character
        CRC and carries no sensor list of its own, so the bytes cannot be
        interpreted without the matching <code>&lt;crc&gt;.cac</code>. There is
        no partial or approximate reading of a file whose cache is missing, so
        the decoder names the one it wants rather than failing vaguely. Caches
        live in the glider's <code>cache</code> directory and in the deployment
        directory the dockserver writes; select them alongside the data, or
        select the whole deployment folder. Once supplied they are kept in this
        browser, so it is a one-time step.
      </p>

      <h2>What it produces</h2>

      <h3>Rows, and the one choice that is yours</h3>
      <p>
        There is no single time base in a Slocum file. Each sensor is written
        on its own subset of cycles — the flight computer logs depth every few
        seconds and position only when the glider surfaces, and the science
        computer keeps its own clock — so making a rectangular table is a
        decision rather than a formatting step.
      </p>
      <p>
        The default is the lossless one: one row per time <em>any</em> sensor
        reported, blank where a sensor did not. Nothing is invented and nothing
        is dropped, and the table is mostly blank, which is an honest picture
        of what a glider logs. The alternative interpolates everything onto one
        sensor's times, which is dense and immediately usable and in which
        every value but the base column is a number the glider never recorded.
        The decoder will do it; it will not do it without being asked.
      </p>

      <h3>Both computers, one table</h3>
      <p>
        Flight and science files merge on one axis: both stamp epoch seconds
        and the science clock is set from the flight computer's. Where a sensor
        is written by <em>both</em> — <code>sci_water_pressure</code> is
        measured by the science computer and relayed to the flight computer at
        a much slower rate — the two stay separate columns, suffixed with the
        file family they came from. They are not the same record and merging
        them would hide that.
      </p>

      <h3>Seawater properties</h3>
      <p>
        A Slocum CTD writes conductivity, in-situ temperature and pressure.
        Ticking the box adds practical salinity, Absolute Salinity,
        Conservative Temperature, density, σ<sub>0</sub> and sound speed,
        computed with the same full-precision <a href="/data/seawater/">TEOS-10 engine</a> as the seawater
        calculator. They are marked as derived in the CSV heading and in the
        netCDF attributes, and without a position or the anomaly atlas the
        salinity column is named <code>salinity_reference</code> rather than <code>salinity_absolute</code>, because that is what it is.
      </p>

      <h3>CSV and netCDF</h3>
      <p>
        The CSV carries data alone, with no comment lines, so it opens
        anywhere. The netCDF is <strong>netCDF-3 classic</strong>, written
        directly rather than through a library, and it is the one that carries
        provenance: the source files, the decode history and every note the
        table produced. Its units are the glider's own unit strings —
        <code>degc</code>, <code>nodim</code>, <code>enum</code> — carried
        verbatim, so it does not claim CF conventions it does not meet. The
        time variable is the exception, and is genuinely udunits.
      </p>

      <h3>Where it stops</h3>
      <p>
        It reads files; it does not process them. There is no thermal-lag
        correction, no despiking, no quality control and no gridding, and it
        does not write the IOOS Glider DAC's trajectory format. Position is
        converted from NMEA and otherwise left as the glider dead-reckoned it.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is checked</h2>
      <p>
        Against <a href="https://github.com/smerckel/dbdreader">dbdreader</a>,
        value for value: every sensor's times and values are compared by a
        SHA-256 over their raw bytes, for a matched flight/science pair from a
        real deployment. The decoder is a port of <a href="https://github.com/oceansensing/SlocumIO.jl">SlocumIO.jl</a>,
        which was itself validated against dbdreader, so agreeing with one is
        agreeing with both. The netCDF writer was separately confirmed by
        reading its output with <code>scipy.io.netcdf_file</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is one glider and one deployment, which is the honest limit of it.
        The format has no specification to check against instead.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

